1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,620
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,640 --> 00:00:07,860
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:08,260 --> 00:00:14,931
This is Lecture 11, the mechanisms of typical special machines.

4
00:00:15,331 --> 00:00:19,100
The buttonholes on a shirt, the buttons sewn on it,

5
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:22,072
the bartacks at the ends of a belt loop,

6
00:00:22,092 --> 00:00:25,564
the zigzag that holds the elastic in underwear.

7
00:00:25,584 --> 00:00:30,393
All of them come from special machines. They look very different,

8
00:00:30,413 --> 00:00:33,662
but they share one structure. A sewing head,

9
00:00:33,682 --> 00:00:37,600
plus a motion of the needle or the fabric in a plane.

10
00:00:37,620 --> 00:00:40,201
Today we follow the zigzag machine.

11
00:00:40,221 --> 00:00:44,138
Where in the turn of the shaft does the swing belong,

12
00:00:44,158 --> 00:00:48,671
which motion law should it follow, and what limits the bight?

13
00:00:50,191 --> 00:00:51,491
The shop-floor problem.

14
00:00:51,891 --> 00:00:59,033
An underwear factory sews elastic on an electronic zigzag machine:

15
00:00:59,053 --> 00:01:02,397
8 millimetre bight, 5000 r/min,

16
00:01:02,417 --> 00:01:09,776
the needle swings from 275 to 85 degrees of shaft angle, 170 in all,

17
00:01:09,796 --> 00:01:13,791
with a modified-trapezoid motion law.

18
00:01:14,191 --> 00:01:21,455
The frame's mass referred to the needle is about 150 grams,

19
00:01:21,475 --> 00:01:28,492
and the sideways inertia force is limited to 250 newtons,

20
00:01:28,512 --> 00:01:31,208
an illustrative value.

21
00:01:31,608 --> 00:01:36,516
The process engineer proposes the smoother cycloidal law,

22
00:01:36,536 --> 00:01:42,222
and squeezing the swing to 120 degrees to give the feed more time.

23
00:01:42,622 --> 00:01:43,722
Will it work?

24
00:01:44,122 --> 00:01:48,622
And how far apart are the catching moments at the two ends?

25
00:01:50,142 --> 00:01:53,739
Special machines: a head plus planar motion.

26
00:01:55,259 --> 00:02:01,727
Every special machine obeys one hard rule: while the needle is in the fabric,

27
00:02:01,747 --> 00:02:07,119
needle and fabric must not move sideways relative to each other.

28
00:02:07,519 --> 00:02:11,321
Break it and the needle bends, stitches go astray,

29
00:02:11,341 --> 00:02:14,379
or the needle hits the plate and breaks.

30
00:02:14,779 --> 00:02:20,919
This is Virtual Lab 11-1, with the swing interval opening and closing.

31
00:02:21,319 --> 00:02:27,264
Too wide, and the swing runs into the time the needle is in the fabric:

32
00:02:27,284 --> 00:02:28,860
the path turns red.

33
00:02:29,260 --> 00:02:33,603
Too narrow, and the same bight is crammed into fewer degrees,

34
00:02:33,623 --> 00:02:36,536
so the inertia force goes over the limit.

35
00:02:38,056 --> 00:02:42,556
The zigzag machine: when and how to swing.

36
00:02:44,076 --> 00:02:48,330
The zigzag stitch 304 is still a lockstitch;

37
00:02:48,350 --> 00:02:52,895
the interlocks simply alternate left and right,

38
00:02:52,915 --> 00:02:59,598
so it stretches more than a straight 301 and suits elastic and knits.

39
00:02:59,998 --> 00:03:05,658
The needle bar sits in a frame that swings about a pivot at its top.

40
00:03:06,058 --> 00:03:11,960
On mechanical machines a zigzag cam drives it through a lever and link,

41
00:03:11,980 --> 00:03:16,715
and changing the link's working radius changes the bight;

42
00:03:16,735 --> 00:03:21,636
electronic machines drive it directly with a stepper motor,

43
00:03:21,656 --> 00:03:26,558
up to 8 millimetres at 5000 stitches a minute on one model.

44
00:03:26,958 --> 00:03:32,628
On the right, the bight sweeps between 2 and 10 millimetres.

45
00:03:34,147 --> 00:03:40,144
The sideways swing has to fit into the part of the turn when the needle is

46
00:03:40,164 --> 00:03:41,607
out of the fabric.

47
00:03:42,007 --> 00:03:45,008
In the worked example,

48
00:03:45,028 --> 00:03:52,971
with a 31-millimetre stroke and 1.5 millimetres of fabric,

49
00:03:52,991 --> 00:04:03,543
the needle leaves the surface at 258.3 degrees and touches it again at 101.7.

50
00:04:03,943 --> 00:04:11,383
Require the point to be at least 0.5 millimetres clear while swinging,

51
00:04:11,403 --> 00:04:17,243
and the usable window is 260 to 100 degrees, about 200.

52
00:04:17,643 --> 00:04:23,038
Note that the feed happens in this same window, shared with the swing,

53
00:04:23,058 --> 00:04:25,358
and thicker fabric narrows it.

54
00:04:26,878 --> 00:04:31,847
Same 8-millimetre bight, same 170 degrees,

55
00:04:31,867 --> 00:04:36,599
but the swing can follow different laws.

56
00:04:36,999 --> 00:04:43,083
Harmonic: the acceleration is a cosine, jumping at both ends,

57
00:04:43,103 --> 00:04:46,485
which causes shocks and vibration.

58
00:04:46,885 --> 00:04:53,404
Cycloidal: the acceleration is a sine, zero at both ends, very smooth,

59
00:04:53,424 --> 00:04:55,833
but with the highest peak.

60
00:04:56,233 --> 00:05:01,889
Modified trapezoid: a flat-topped acceleration, continuous at both ends,

61
00:05:01,909 --> 00:05:04,964
with a peak about the same as harmonic.

62
00:05:05,364 --> 00:05:09,866
The three laws are cycling now; watch how the lower curve,

63
00:05:09,886 --> 00:05:12,984
the needle's sideways position, changes.

64
00:05:14,504 --> 00:05:22,087
The peak acceleration is C times the bight w times the shaft angular speed squared,

65
00:05:22,107 --> 00:05:26,300
divided by the square of the swing angle beta.

66
00:05:26,700 --> 00:05:36,692
C depends on the motion law: about 4.93 harmonic, 2 pi or about 6.28 cycloidal,

67
00:05:36,712 --> 00:05:40,494
about 4.89 modified trapezoid.

68
00:05:40,894 --> 00:05:45,197
So acceleration goes with the bight and with speed squared,

69
00:05:45,217 --> 00:05:48,347
and inversely with the swing angle squared.

70
00:05:48,747 --> 00:05:53,568
The cycloid the engineer calls smoother is smooth at the ends,

71
00:05:53,588 --> 00:05:55,754
but its peak is the highest;

72
00:05:55,774 --> 00:06:01,767
the modified trapezoid peaks about 22 percent lower and has the best of both.

73
00:06:03,287 --> 00:06:05,016
Both ends must be caught.

74
00:06:06,536 --> 00:06:09,655
The hook shaft runs front to back,

75
00:06:09,675 --> 00:06:14,918
so as the hook point passes the needle it moves sideways.

76
00:06:15,318 --> 00:06:17,977
With the needle at the two ends,

77
00:06:17,997 --> 00:06:23,754
the point has to turn an extra w over R to get from one to the other;

78
00:06:23,774 --> 00:06:27,271
the hook turns twice as fast as the shaft,

79
00:06:27,291 --> 00:06:32,964
so the shaft angle differs by half that: delta phi equals w over 2R.

80
00:06:33,364 --> 00:06:42,899
With R 13.5 millimetres and an 8-millimetre bight, that's about 17 degrees.

81
00:06:43,299 --> 00:06:48,916
The point sweeps right to left, so the right end is caught first,

82
00:06:48,936 --> 00:06:55,681
when the needle has risen only about 1.1 millimetres, against 3.8 at the left:

83
00:06:55,701 --> 00:06:58,023
nearly 3 millimetres apart.

84
00:06:59,543 --> 00:07:04,337
Both ends must be caught while the loop is big enough,

85
00:07:04,357 --> 00:07:06,655
and that limits the bight.

86
00:07:07,055 --> 00:07:15,834
With Lecture 5's hook window, a rise of about 1.5 to 2.8 millimetres,

87
00:07:15,854 --> 00:07:21,955
the maximum bight is only about 4.0 millimetres.

88
00:07:22,355 --> 00:07:27,871
With Lecture 10's illustrative criterion, 1.0 to 4.2,

89
00:07:27,891 --> 00:07:32,467
the two ends can differ by about 20 degrees,

90
00:07:32,487 --> 00:07:36,123
which allows about 9.5 millimetres.

91
00:07:36,523 --> 00:07:38,805
The manual goes up to 10,

92
00:07:38,825 --> 00:07:45,344
so the real loop window is clearly much wider than the Lecture 5 model;

93
00:07:45,364 --> 00:07:47,370
it has to be measured.

94
00:07:48,890 --> 00:07:53,148
Buttonholes, buttons, bartacks and embroidery.

95
00:07:54,668 --> 00:07:56,888
Buttonholers come in two kinds.

96
00:07:57,288 --> 00:08:04,207
The straight buttonhole on shirts is 304 zigzag: one row of zigzag, a bartack,

97
00:08:04,227 --> 00:08:09,989
the other row and the other bartack, then the knife cuts it open,

98
00:08:10,009 --> 00:08:11,590
five steps in all.

99
00:08:11,990 --> 00:08:17,810
Sewing first and then cutting gives neat edges; cutting first frays less.

100
00:08:18,210 --> 00:08:23,245
The keyhole buttonhole on suits, coats and jeans is 404,

101
00:08:23,265 --> 00:08:29,743
a single-needle two-thread zigzag chainstitch wrapped round a gimp cord;

102
00:08:29,763 --> 00:08:36,242
around the round end, needle and looper have to rotate about its centre.

103
00:08:37,762 --> 00:08:42,825
Mechanical button sewers and bartackers are built alike:

104
00:08:42,845 --> 00:08:48,906
the shaft drives a pattern cam through a large worm-gear reduction,

105
00:08:48,926 --> 00:08:53,262
and one turn of the cam is one complete pattern.

106
00:08:53,662 --> 00:09:00,558
Two grooves on the cam drive the button clamp or work clamp through X and Y levers.

107
00:09:00,958 --> 00:09:04,870
Each step of the cam moves the clamp one stitch,

108
00:09:04,890 --> 00:09:11,177
and the rising part of every step must line up with the angle when the needle

109
00:09:11,197 --> 00:09:12,898
is out of the fabric.

110
00:09:13,298 --> 00:09:21,128
Button sewers often use a single-thread 101 chainstitch with an anti-unravel device;

111
00:09:21,148 --> 00:09:26,641
bartackers use lockstitch, an underlay first, then a cover.

112
00:09:28,161 --> 00:09:33,917
A multi-needle quilter carries dozens of needles on one beam,

113
00:09:33,937 --> 00:09:40,261
each with its own looper, sewing many parallel rows of 401 at once.

114
00:09:40,661 --> 00:09:44,579
An embroidery head has 9 to 15 needle bars;

115
00:09:44,599 --> 00:09:48,883
one driver works only the bar lined up with it,

116
00:09:48,903 --> 00:09:54,652
and to change colour the whole needle-bar case shifts sideways.

117
00:09:55,052 --> 00:10:00,278
Multi-head machines share upper and lower shafts.

118
00:10:00,678 --> 00:10:06,161
Embroidery machines run at only about a quarter of lockstitch speed,

119
00:10:06,181 --> 00:10:08,589
about 1200 r/min on one model,

120
00:10:08,609 --> 00:10:14,254
because the frame is heavy and every move of a few millimetres must be

121
00:10:14,274 --> 00:10:18,058
finished while the needle is out of the fabric.

122
00:10:19,578 --> 00:10:23,594
Back to the shop-floor problem, with numbers.

123
00:10:23,994 --> 00:10:34,401
The shaft turns at 524 radians per second, and 170 degrees is 2.97 radians.

124
00:10:34,801 --> 00:10:42,646
Modified trapezoid: a peak of about 1218 metres per second squared;

125
00:10:42,666 --> 00:10:47,341
times 0.15 kilograms, about 183 newtons.

126
00:10:47,741 --> 00:10:55,868
Cycloidal: about 1565, giving about 235 newtons.

127
00:10:56,268 --> 00:10:58,568
Both are under 250.

128
00:10:58,968 --> 00:11:09,427
Squeeze to 120 degrees and the acceleration is multiplied by 170 over 120 squared,

129
00:11:09,447 --> 00:11:17,862
about 2.0: about 471 newtons cycloidal and 367 modified trapezoid,

130
00:11:17,882 --> 00:11:20,418
both over the limit.

131
00:11:21,937 --> 00:11:28,743
Choosing a swing law is the time-scaling step of robot trajectory planning:

132
00:11:28,763 --> 00:11:31,837
to cover a distance w in a time T,

133
00:11:31,857 --> 00:11:36,297
the peak acceleration goes with w over T squared,

134
00:11:36,317 --> 00:11:42,576
so halving the time quadruples the acceleration and the motor torque.

135
00:11:42,976 --> 00:11:48,005
The S-curve profiles of servo drives, which limit jerk,

136
00:11:48,025 --> 00:11:54,613
follow the same idea as the modified trapezoid: continuous acceleration,

137
00:11:54,633 --> 00:11:57,367
no shock, and a moderate peak.

138
00:11:57,767 --> 00:12:04,892
A dispensing or welding robot may make big sideways moves only while its tool

139
00:12:04,912 --> 00:12:06,841
is off the workpiece:

140
00:12:06,861 --> 00:12:13,707
the same rule as a needle that may swing only when it's out of the fabric.

141
00:12:15,227 --> 00:12:17,847
The same rule shows up at home.

142
00:12:18,247 --> 00:12:21,507
Stapling a row along the edge of a sheet,

143
00:12:21,527 --> 00:12:27,107
you can slide the paper to the next spot only while the stapler is up;

144
00:12:27,127 --> 00:12:31,826
move it while pressing down and the staple goes in crooked.

145
00:12:32,226 --> 00:12:33,530
The zigzag needle,

146
00:12:33,550 --> 00:12:39,195
the button clamp and the embroidery frame likewise may move only in the short

147
00:12:39,215 --> 00:12:41,916
time the needle is out of the fabric.

148
00:12:43,436 --> 00:12:44,596
Back to the start.

149
00:12:44,996 --> 00:12:52,928
Modified trapezoid gives about 183 newtons and the cycloid about 235,

150
00:12:52,948 --> 00:12:59,496
both under 250: the switch is possible, with less margin.

151
00:12:59,896 --> 00:13:08,767
Squeezed to 120 degrees, about 471 cycloidal and 367 modified trapezoid,

152
00:13:08,787 --> 00:13:11,236
both over the limit.

153
00:13:11,636 --> 00:13:18,594
And the feed and the swing already happen together in the same out-of-fabric window,

154
00:13:18,614 --> 00:13:23,163
so a shorter swing gives the feed no extra time at all.

155
00:13:23,563 --> 00:13:31,115
The two ends are caught 8 over 27 radians apart, about 17 degrees,

156
00:13:31,135 --> 00:13:32,951
right end first.

157
00:13:34,471 --> 00:13:39,212
Now Virtual lab 11-1, zigzag swing timing and loop catching.

158
00:13:39,232 --> 00:13:43,021
On the WenQuest platform you can do it yourself.

159
00:13:43,041 --> 00:13:45,719
The readings of the four criteria,

160
00:13:45,739 --> 00:13:50,321
the values of the settings and the tasks are blurred here,

161
00:13:50,341 --> 00:13:53,971
so you can read them yourself on the platform.

162
00:13:54,371 --> 00:13:59,698
First set the swing start to 250 degrees and the end to 110.

163
00:13:59,718 --> 00:14:04,421
A red segment appears on the needle path on the left:

164
00:14:04,441 --> 00:14:09,768
the swing has run into the time the needle is in the fabric.

165
00:14:10,168 --> 00:14:13,530
Task one: press Scramble. The interval,

166
00:14:13,550 --> 00:14:17,604
the law and the hook timing are all thrown off.

167
00:14:17,624 --> 00:14:24,280
Adjust the swing start and end first, until the swing leaves the red segment,

168
00:14:24,300 --> 00:14:28,701
then the hook timing, until all four criteria pass.

169
00:14:29,102 --> 00:14:34,520
Reset, and switch the motion law to cycloidal. On the platform,

170
00:14:34,540 --> 00:14:39,269
try modified trapezoid, cycloidal and harmonic in turn.

171
00:14:39,289 --> 00:14:42,031
Note the sideways inertia force,

172
00:14:42,051 --> 00:14:46,694
divide by 0.15 kilograms to get the peak acceleration,

173
00:14:46,714 --> 00:14:49,802
and compare with the worked example.

174
00:14:50,202 --> 00:14:56,111
Task two: with the cycloidal law, narrow the interval step by step.

175
00:14:56,131 --> 00:15:02,837
Find the narrowest interval that keeps the force at or under 250 newtons and

176
00:15:02,857 --> 00:15:06,112
the swing entirely out of the fabric.

177
00:15:06,132 --> 00:15:09,917
Read it yourself and put it in your report.

178
00:15:10,317 --> 00:15:13,267
Finally, reset and slowly widen the bight.

179
00:15:13,287 --> 00:15:18,642
Watch how crosses for missed catches appear in the stitch view on the right.

180
00:15:18,662 --> 00:15:21,683
This only shows the effect. For task three,

181
00:15:21,703 --> 00:15:25,290
first set the hook timing to the top of the window,

182
00:15:25,310 --> 00:15:29,958
then find the maximum bight and enter it in the task box to check.

